35:| Normative Modeling  ........................................................................................................  14                    |
169:T1-weighted  MRI  images  were  preprocessed  using  a  voxel-based  morphometry  pipeline (VBM) based on the CAT12 package (http://www.neuro.uni-jena.de/cat/) in SPM12. Images were first segmented into grey-matter, white matter and cerebrospinal fluid. Next, we created a study-specific template with DARTEL  (i.e.,  a  nonlinear diffeomorphic  registration algorithm 40 ) and segmented images were registered to MNI space. Flow fields from a Jacobian modulation were used to preserve  the  information  on  local  tissue  volume.  Smoothing  was performed with a 4mm full-width half-max (FWHM) isotropic Gaussian kernel. Features for subsequent normative modeling were VBM-derived, voxel-wise GM volumes per individual restricted to the right and left FFG ROIs.
179:fMRI data analysis followed standard processing routines in SPM12 (http://www.fil.ion.ucl.ac.uk/spm/),  including  slice-time  correction,  a  two-step  realignment procedure, unified segmentation and normalization to standard stereotactic space as defined by the  Montreal Neurological Institute (MNI), and smoothing with an 8mm FWHM Gaussian kernel. For each subject, task conditions were modelled as boxcar functions that accounted for the presentation of face blocks and shape blocks, respectively. Task regressors were convolved with the canonical hemodynamic response function (HRF) and subjected as predictors to a general  linear  model  (GLM),  along  with  six  realignment  parameters  to  account  for  head motion. During first-level model estimation, data was high-pass filtered with a cut-off of 256s, and  an  autoregressive  model  of  the  first  order  was  applied.  The  faces  condition  was subsequently  contrasted  to  the  shapes  condition  to  identify  brain  responses  reflecting sensitivity to faces. T-contrast maps per individual (restricted to the right and left FFG ROIs) were the features for subsequent normative modeling.
181:We used the same smoothing kernels as those employed in previous studies using normative modeling 44-47 . While individual-level analyses typically use minimal smoothing, we employed smoothing kernels consistent with traditional group average designs in this study for several reasons. Smoothing helps to increase the signal-to-noise ratio, make the data more normally distributed and account for anatomical and functional variability across individuals. This is essential for subsequent post-hoc group comparisons and for achieving spatial overlap with anatomical and functional atlases.
199:within the FFG, we conducted seed-based correlation analysis. For this, the mean time series was  extracted  from  a  spherical  region  of  interest  (6mm  in  diameter)  centered  in  each participant's peak activation voxel within the fusiform face area (FFA). This peak voxel was identified based on the individual Hariri task-fMRI T-maps within the mid-lateral FFG (mFus) and posterior lateral FFG (pFus), which correspond to the FFA that are part of the probabilistic functional  atlas  of  human  occipito-temporal  visual  cortex  area 50 .  While  prior  studies acknowledge the existence of these two subregions 50 , their differential functional implications remain to be established 51-53 . To ensure that we did not exclude any potentially relevant faceselective voxels and account for the fact that most prior studies have identified at least two face-selective regions on the FFG, we included both sub-regions for peak identification. By identifying individual peaks for each participant, we ensured that our methodology not only acknowledges  but  also  leverages  the  inherent  differences  among  participants.  Next,  to characterize the fine-grained functional subdivisions within the FFG in the context of face processing,  seed-based  correlation  analysis  was  performed  between  the  timeseries  derived from each individual's peak activation voxel within the FFA and the remaining voxels within the FFG. Next, values were Fisher's z-transformed and finally smoothed with a 3D Gaussian kernel  (FWHM=6mm).  This  FFA-connectivity  was  the  feature  for  subsequent  normative modeling.
205:estimate topographies per individual across the 18 parcels per hemisphere was derived. The PC across these time series was used as the feature for subsequent normative modeling.
207:## Normative Modeling
209:Normative modelling is an emerging statistical technique that allows parsing heterogeneity by charting  variation  in  brain-behaviour  mappings  relative  to  a  normative  range  and  provides statistical inference at the level of the individual 58 . The term 'normative' should not be seen as incompatible with the neurodiversity framework as it simply refers to statistical norms such as growth charts that vary by demographics such as age and sex. Variation from the norm is part of neurodiversity. Prior research shows that modelling cortical features as deviations from a normative neurodevelopmental trajectory and incorporating individual neurobiological variations  provides  more  sensitive  measure  to  parse  heterogeneity 58 and  map  multimodal signatures in psychopathology 59 while also improving predictive performance 60 .  Prior  work addressing multimodal signatures of face processing in conduct disorder has validated this approach and shows that modelling deviations before merging different modalities is more sensitive than using raw features 59 . As in prior work 44,59 , normative models 58,61,62 were trained across all individuals using Bayesian Linear Regression (BLR) 63 using PCNtoolkit package (version 0.26)  (https://pcntoolkit.readthedocs.io/en/) modelling the relationship between each brain imaging modality (within the right and left FFG ROI) and age, sex and scanning site. Normative models were derived in an unbiased manner under 10-fold cross-validation. This Bayesian approach calculates the probability distribution over all functions that fit the data while specifying a prior over all possible values and relocating probabilities based on evidence (i.e., observed data). As such, it yields unbiased estimates of generalizability and inferences with increasing uncertainty with fewer data. In primary analysis, a B-spline basis expansion of the  of  covariate  vector  was  used  to  model  non-linear  effects  of  age.  We  compared  model performance  with  that  of  modelling  age  linearly  (without  a  B-spline  basis  expansion).  To estimate  voxel-wise/time-point-wise  deviations  for  each  modality  in  each  individual,  we derived normative probability maps (NPM) that quantify the deviation from the normative model summarized in Z scores. These indicate the difference between the prediction (mean, ! " ij )  at  each  brain  location  (j)  and  true  brain  value  (y ij )  scaled  by  the  prediction  variance [expected level of variation σ 2 ij and variance learned from the normative distribution (σ 2 nj )]:
213:The accuracy of the normative model was evaluated using the correlation between the true and the predicted voxel values (Rho), the mean standardized log-loss, the explained variance and standardized mean squared error (Supplementary Fig. 1). We further estimated the forward models  to  depict  the  spatial  /  temporal  representation  of  the  voxel-wise  /  time  point-wise normative model. For this, we identified the peak activation within the FFA based on the group T-map from the Hariri task and plotted the normative model within this peak voxel. For EEG, it was plotted at 170ms. Given that the categorical covariates (sex, site) will render slightly different slopes, here, we plotted the trajectories in males and in the largest acquisition site (KCL) for descriptive purposes only. Extended Data Fig. 1 depicts these normative models per modality.
251:To test for the potential influence of handedness on our results, we ran several control analyses. 1)  We  computed  Spearman's  correlations  between  the  handedness  scores  and  the  subject loadings  of  each  independent  component  -  both  across  the  entire  sample  and  within  each diagnostic group separately. There were no significant associations between the handedness scores and the subject loading of each independent component in either group (Supplementary Table 3). 2) We additionally included handedness as a covariate when comparing autistic and non-autistic individuals on the subject loadings of all ICs and within the CCA analysis. Results remained unchanged (group difference IC44: t =3.5, pFDR =0.28; CCA: r =0.65, pFDR =0.002). 3) Finally, we also excluded left-handed and ambidextrous individuals from the two groups and  re-ran  the  group  comparison  and  CCA  analyses.  Results  remained  unchanged  (group difference IC44: t =3.7, pFDR =0.01; CCA: r =0.66, pFDR =0.002).
304:45. Rutherford, S. et al. Evidence for embracing normative modeling. eLife 12 , e85082 (2023).
324:63. Fraza, C. J., Dinga, R., Beckmann, C. F. &amp; Marquand, A. F. Warped Bayesian linear regression for normative modelling of big data. NeuroImage 245 , 118715 (2021).
326:65. Wolfers, T. et al. Individual differences v. the average patient: Mapping the heterogeneity in ADHD using normative models. Psychological Medicine 50 , 314-323 (2019).
344:Supplementary Fig. 1. Evaluation metrics for normative models across modalities (nonlinear age effects). Evaluation of normative model performance for each imaging modality per hemisphere when using BLR with B-Spline to model non-linear age effects. The upper row shows the correlation between the true and predicted values (Rho; closer to 1 = better model performance), the second row shows the mean standardized log-loss (MSLL; more negative = better model performance), the third row shows the standardized mean squared error (SMSE; closer to 0 = better model performance) and the last row shows the explained variance (EXPV; closer to 1 = better model performance). Abbreviations : MSLL= mean standardized log-loss, SMSE=standardized mean squared error, EXPV=explained variance.
350:Supplementary Fig. 2 . Evaluation metrics for normative models across modalities (linear age-effects). Evaluation  of  normative  model  performance  for  each  imaging  modality  per hemisphere when using BLR without a B-Spline basis extension to model linear age effects. Evaluations metrics are highly similar to those when including a B-Spline basis extension. The upper row shows the correlation between the true and predicted values (Rho; closer to 1 = better  model  performance),  the  second  row  shows  the  mean  standardized  log-loss  (MSLL; more negative = better model performance), the third row shows the standardized mean squared error (SMSE; closer to 0 = better model performance) and the last row shows the explained variance  (EXPV;  closer  to  1  =  better  model  performance). Abbreviations :  MSLL=  mean standardized log-loss, SMSE=standardized mean squared error, EXPV=explained variance.

